/-
# ETOL LANGUAGE MEMBERSHIP for a fixed grammar is NP-complete

Source:
M. R. Garey and D. S. Johnson, *Computers and Intractability: A Guide to the
Theory of NP-Completeness*, W. H. Freeman (1979), problem [AL19] ETOL LANGUAGE MEMBERSHIP,
comment ("If `G` is fixed, the problem is in NP and there exist particular grammars for
which it is NP-complete, even if `G` is a "TOL grammar" (has no nonterminals) and is
`ε`-free");
J. van Leeuwen, "The membership question for ETOL-languages is polynomially complete",
Information Processing Letters 3 (1975), 138-143, doi:10.1016/0020-0190(75)90027-7
(Corollary 3.2).
-/
module

public import Batteries.Data.List.Basic
public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import CSLibExt.Computability.CookLevin
public import Mathlib.Logic.Relation
public import MathlibExt.Computability.BinaryNatEncoding
public import MathlibExt.Computability.Complexity

@[expose] public section

namespace MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.ETOLLanguageMembershipFixedGrammar

/-!
# ETOL LANGUAGE MEMBERSHIP for a fixed grammar is NP-complete

An ETOL grammar `G = (N, Σ, {δ₁, ..., δₙ}, S)` has tables `δᵢ`, each a set of productions
taking symbols to strings of symbols; at each step of a derivation one table is chosen and
every symbol of the current string is replaced according to some production of that table,
and the language of `G` is the set of strings over `Σ` derivable from `S`. A TOL grammar has
no nonterminals: every symbol is a terminal, so its language is the set of all strings
derivable from `S`. It is `ε`-free when no production has the empty right-hand side. For a
fixed grammar `G`, ETOL LANGUAGE MEMBERSHIP asks, given a string `w`, whether `w` is in the
language generated by `G`.

The claim stated here is the existence claim of the source: there is a particular `ε`-free
TOL grammar `G` for which this membership problem is NP-complete. The other claim of the
same comment, that the problem is in NP for every fixed ETOL grammar, is not stated here.

The symbols of a TOL grammar with `n` symbols are `0, ..., n - 1`. A table is a list of
productions `(a, w)`, read as the set of its entries; such a production rewrites the symbol
`a` to the string `w`. Following van Leeuwen (1975) and the usual definition of tables as
finite substitutions, every table has at least one production for every symbol.

Encoding: the input string `w` is decoded from a bitstring with the repository's canonical
`MetaMathlibExt.BitstringEncoding`, as a list of natural numbers. Undecodable bitstrings are
no-instances, and so is every string with a letter outside the grammar's symbols, which no
derivation produces.

NP-completeness is stated in the same form as the proved results in
`CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Karp`: membership in
`MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP`, plus a polynomial-time many-one
reduction from every language in `NP`
(`Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed`).
-/

/-- A table: a list of productions `(a, w)`, each rewriting the symbol `a` to the string
`w`. -/
abbrev Table := List (ℕ × List ℕ)

/-- A TOL grammar: the number `n` of symbols (they are `0, ..., n - 1`), the list of tables,
and the initial symbol `S`. -/
abbrev TOLGrammar := ℕ × List Table × ℕ

/-- Well-formedness of a TOL grammar: `S` is a symbol, every production takes a symbol to a
string of symbols, and every table has at least one production for every symbol. -/
def WellFormed : TOLGrammar → Prop
  | (n, ts, S) =>
    S < n ∧ (∀ t ∈ ts, ∀ p ∈ t, p.1 < n ∧ ∀ b ∈ p.2, b < n) ∧
      ∀ t ∈ ts, ∀ a < n, ∃ w, (a, w) ∈ t

/-- The grammar is `ε`-free: no production has the empty right-hand side. -/
def EpsilonFree (G : TOLGrammar) : Prop :=
  ∀ t ∈ G.2.1, ∀ p ∈ t, p.2 ≠ []

/-- One derivation step with the table `t`: every symbol of `u` is replaced according to some
production of `t`, giving `v`. -/
def TableStep (t : Table) (u v : List ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∃ ws : List (List ℕ), List.Forall₂ (fun a w => (a, w) ∈ t) u ws ∧ v = ws.flatten

/-- One derivation step of the grammar: a step with one of its tables. -/
def Step (G : TOLGrammar) (u v : List ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∃ t ∈ G.2.1, TableStep t u v

/-- The language generated by the TOL grammar: all strings derivable from `S` in zero or more
steps. -/
def language (G : TOLGrammar) : Set (List ℕ) :=
  {w | Relation.ReflTransGen (Step G) [G.2.2] w}

open Classical in
/-- ETOL LANGUAGE MEMBERSHIP for the fixed grammar `G` as a decision problem over
bitstrings. -/
noncomputable def membershipDec (G : TOLGrammar) :
    MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.DecisionProblem := fun x =>
  match MetaMathlibExt.BitstringEncoding.bitDecode (α := List ℕ) x with
  | some w => decide (w ∈ language G)
  | none => false

/-- **ETOL LANGUAGE MEMBERSHIP is NP-complete for some fixed grammar, even an `ε`-free TOL
grammar.** Source: Garey and Johnson (1979), [AL19], comment; van Leeuwen (1975),
Corollary 3.2. -/
public theorem_wanted exists_tolGrammar_membership_npComplete :
    ∃ G : TOLGrammar, WellFormed G ∧ EpsilonFree G ∧
      (membershipDec G ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP ∧
        ∀ L : MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.DecisionProblem,
          L ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP →
            Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed L (membershipDec G))

end MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.ETOLLanguageMembershipFixedGrammar
